# BloodLink app environment — copy to `.env` and adjust for your machine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (macOS / Linux)
#   copy .env.example .env      (Windows PowerShell)
#
# `.env` is gitignored: this file is per-developer, and the address below is
# specific to one machine on one network.
#
# Metro inlines every EXPO_PUBLIC_* value at build time, so changing it requires
# restarting the dev server with a cleared cache:
#
#   npx expo start --clear
#
# Only non-secret values belong here — anything prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ is compiled
# into the shipped bundle and is readable by anyone with the app.
#
# Pick the address that matches how you are running:
#
#   Android emulator   http://10.0.2.2:4000        (alias for host loopback)
#   iOS simulator      http://localhost:4000
#   Physical device    http://<your-computer-LAN-IP>:4000
#   Web in a browser   http://localhost:4000
#
# For a physical device the computer and phone must be on the same network, and
# the server must not be bound to loopback. The dev server defaults to HOST=0.0.0.0
# for exactly this reason; pass HOST=127.0.0.1 if you want to restrict it.
# Defaults to the Android emulator address, which is the most common target.
# Switch to one of the others above when testing elsewhere.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:4000
